WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:11.701
A robot pushes a puck across a table. Its camera also sees a patterned wall, and a fan blows across the surface. Which details should its representation keep?

00:00:12.121 --> 00:00:21.464
The puck position matters because the robot can change it. The wind matters because it changes what a push will do. The wallpaper might not matter at all.

00:00:22.214 --> 00:00:35.765
Now animate the wallpaper independently. Millions of pixels change, although nothing about the robot's useful choices has changed. A pixel reconstruction objective still has to explain that motion.

00:00:36.185 --> 00:00:44.409
An objective based on control asks a different question: which differences help predict the effects of what the agent can choose to do?

00:00:45.159 --> 00:00:57.426
Learning to Perceive the World Through Control studies how empowerment objectives shape representations. The target is not simply every moving object, or everything outside the robot.

00:00:57.846 --> 00:01:04.357
The puck, wind, and wallpaper separate controllable state, relevant context, and irrelevant nuisance.

00:01:06.557 --> 00:01:17.180
First, freeze the wind. Choose a leftward or rightward push and watch the puck respond. The chosen action leaves a recognizable effect in the puck position.

00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:29.078
A useful representation should preserve that distinction. If both outcomes are encoded identically, the representation erases control that the robot actually had.

00:01:29.828 --> 00:01:40.084
Next, keep the same push but reverse the wind. The future puck position changes. The robot did not choose the wind, yet needs it to predict the outcome.

00:01:40.504 --> 00:01:51.789
This is the crucial middle category. Uncontrollable does not mean irrelevant. Removing the wind would make the same encoded state and action produce confusing predictions.

00:01:52.539 --> 00:02:03.457
Finally, change only the wallpaper. Keep the puck, wind, and chosen push fixed. The physically relevant transition stays the same despite a very different image.

00:02:03.877 --> 00:02:18.864
The paper's representation framework separates relevant control structure from exogenous structure. Our three-layer picture illustrates why a representation must retain context while ignoring independent visual clutter.

00:02:21.064 --> 00:02:33.618
Imagine two observations with different wallpaper but identical puck position and wind. From either observation, the same action produces the same distribution over relevant futures.

00:02:34.038 --> 00:02:45.087
For predicting those futures, the observations can share a representation. This is the forward-looking idea: compare what can happen next under each available choice.

00:02:45.837 --> 00:02:57.184
Now compare observations with opposite wind. Apply the same rightward push in both. Their future-state distributions differ, even though the initial puck positions match.

00:02:57.604 --> 00:03:08.922
Merging these observations would hide a distinction needed for control. The representation must retain enough context to separate those different consequences of the same action.

00:03:09.672 --> 00:03:21.276
Notice that we compare distributions, not just one sampled outcome. Two noisy transitions can land in the same place once while still following different underlying dynamics.

00:03:21.696 --> 00:03:32.661
A forward equivalence is about matching the appropriate future behavior across actions. A few visually similar examples are not sufficient evidence for that equivalence.

00:03:34.861 --> 00:03:44.133
There is also a backward-looking question. Given an observed outcome, what does it tell us about the earlier state and the action or skill that produced it?

00:03:44.553 --> 00:03:54.963
This is the decoding direction we saw in DIAYN. The final puck position can be evidence about a preceding push, even before we name a downstream task.

00:03:55.713 --> 00:04:07.840
Two outcomes are equivalent in this direction when they carry the same relevant predecessor evidence. Changing independent wallpaper should not suddenly identify a different preceding skill.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:18.660
But moving the puck may change that evidence. The forward and backward views preserve different aspects of the same relationship between choices and their consequences.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:33.051
The paper combines information objectives with minimality conditions to characterize which distinctions a representation retains. Minimality prevents it from simply keeping every irrelevant detail too.

00:04:33.471 --> 00:04:45.545
These are results under specified assumptions and optimality conditions. They do not say that an arbitrary neural encoder automatically discovers the desired factors during training.

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:55.542
Let a blue skill move the puck left and a yellow skill move it right. Add a background light that independently flashes on or off.

00:04:55.962 --> 00:05:05.007
Each skill now has twice as many visible outcomes. Yet after seeing the puck, the light gives no additional evidence about which skill was chosen.

00:05:05.757 --> 00:05:18.296
Write the information as a log ratio: the outcome probability under a skill, divided by its probability before knowing the skill. The independent light contributes the same factor to both.

00:05:18.716 --> 00:05:29.093
Those factors cancel. So the exact mutual information is unchanged, even though the full observation has more entropy and contains more varying pixels.

00:05:29.843 --> 00:05:39.411
Now connect the light to the wind, or let it reveal the selected skill. The independence assumption breaks, and that cancellation may no longer apply.

00:05:39.831 --> 00:05:45.315
Test the assumption: does this feature change dynamics, or reveal the selected skill?

00:05:47.515 --> 00:05:59.365
A useful audit changes one part of the scene at a time. Change wallpaper only: the control representation should remain stable when that variation is genuinely irrelevant.

00:05:59.785 --> 00:06:11.347
Change wind instead: predictions should adjust because the dynamics changed. Good invariance preserves the distinctions needed for action while removing the ones that do not help.

00:06:12.097 --> 00:06:22.048
Then test actual behavior. Can a policy using the representation still select different puck outcomes? Does it adapt correctly when the wind changes?

00:06:22.468 --> 00:06:29.221
Test control and prediction directly; a tidy embedding alone does not establish either capability.

00:06:29.971 --> 00:06:42.738
Return to the three layers. Puck position records a controllable effect. Wind supplies necessary context. Independent wallpaper contributes detail without changing the control problem.

00:06:43.158 --> 00:06:50.007
The assumptions support the theory; interventions let us investigate these distinctions experimentally.
